  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Another pleasant day is expected across the region today \nas high pressure continues to bring a period of drier weather. High \ntemperatures will be a few degrees warmer today under light \nsoutherly flow with daytime highs climbing into the 80s areawide. \n\nHeading into the latter half of the week, warm and unsettled weather \nis expected as the region remains sandwiched between high pressure \nto the south and an upper level trough to the north, with zonal flow \nbringing several different disturbances to the region. With broad \nsouthwesterly flow ushering in plenty of moisture, chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will be possible. While there doesn't look \nto be any real defined boundary to help drive shower activity, the \nshortwave activity Thursday and Friday may provide enough energy to \ndrive afternoon shower and thunderstorm chances. If ingredients \nalign, there could be the potential for some stronger thunderstorms \nboth afternoons, but generally more garden-variety thunderstorms can \nbe expected. Shower activity is expected to wane towards the \nevenings with loss of diurnal heating. High temperatures Thursday \nwill rise into the mid to upper 80s with dewpoints around 70 \ndegrees, resulting in heat indices in the upper 80s to around 90.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A Bermuda High will be remain in place Sunday through \nTuesday, which will keep warm, moist flow progressing into the \nregion while various systems travel west-to-east in quick zonal flow \naloft. A more appreciable trough will drive southeastwards on \nTuesday, and that will break down the Bermuda High and shunt the \nplume of moisture south and east of our region. Some forecast \nguidance still suggests there's a pocket of dry air in there \nsomewhere between systems on Sunday, but the timing of this still \nremains a little uncertain. Any potential for stronger storms will \ndepend on the time of day that these periodic 700mb troughs push \neast. If they are timed during peak heating, the warm, moist \nenvironment and gradually increasing shear suggests the ingredients \ncould briefly materialize. Some of the experimental machine learning \nensembles have some low end probabilities, but a greater focus \nsouth. So will keep an eye out, but any potential for stronger \nstorms will be contingent on the timing of synoptic forcing. After \nTuesday, we'll be under the influence of a broad upper low, bringing \ntemperatures near to just below normal. However, there's still \nseveral embedded systems. So showers could remain in the forecast \nmid to late next week, but thunderstorm chances will minimize.",
